You are given eight inputs: the target table name, the target table description, the target table columns names, the target table
column descriptions, the candidate table name, the candidate table description, all candidate table columns names and all
candidate table columns descriptions.

Your task is to classify if the two tables are contextually similar, it means their descriptions are related or talk about similar topics,
then if they do identify attributes with similar semantics so that they could be used to join the two tables. Answer only with the appropriate
information in the specified JSON format.

Definition of Joinability In relational databases, a JOIN combines rows from two tables based on matching column values that
represent the same real-world entity.

Two tables are considered joinable if they contain at least one column (or semantically equivalent columns) whose values can be
matched.

Instructions
Carefully read the target table name.
Carefully read the target table description.
Carefully read the target table columns names.
Carefully read the target table columns description.
Carefully read the candidate table name.
Carefully read the candidate table description.
Carefully read the candidate table columns names.
Carefully read the candidate table columns descriptions.
Determine if the two tables exist within the same contextual domain. If they clearly belong to different domains, they are not
joinable, even if column names appear similar.
Identify whether there is evidence of at least one shared or semantically equivalent column from the candidate table with the target
table columns.
Answer with the most appropriate pair of columns found in the target table and candidate table.

Answer only with:
{  "table_joinable" : "Yes" | "No",
"column_joinable": "Yes" | "No",
"target_match" : [target_column_names] | null
"candidate_match": [candidate_column_names] | null}

The output must be a single JSON object with exactly four fields:

table_joinable - a string that must be "Yes" or "No".
"Yes" if both tables exists within the same contextual domain.
"No" if the tables are not contextually related.

column_joinable – a string that must be "Yes" or "No".
"Yes" if at least one column in the candidate table matches or semantically corresponds to a target table's column.
"No" if no column in the candidate table refers to the same entity any of the target table columns.

target_match - the target table column names that are semantically equivalent to a candidate table columns.
If column_joinable is "Yes", specify the exact name of the columns in the target table that matches the candidate table column.
If column_joinable is "No", return null.

candidate_match – the candidate table column names that are semantically equivalent to the target table columns.
If column_joinable is "Yes", specify the exact name of the columns in the candidate table that matches the target table column.
If column_joinable is "No", return null.

